## Making Services Stateless — Prerequisite for Horizontal Scaling

```java
// STATEFUL (BAD for horizontal scaling):
@RestController
class OrderController {
    private Map<String, User> sessionStore = new HashMap<>();  // In-memory!

    @PostMapping("/login")
    public String login(@RequestBody LoginRequest req) {
        User user = authenticate(req);
        String sessionId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        sessionStore.put(sessionId, user);  // Stored in Instance A's memory
        return sessionId;
    }

    @GetMapping("/orders")
    public List<Order> getOrders(@RequestHeader("Session-Id") String sessionId) {
        User user = sessionStore.get(sessionId);  // Only works if Instance A!
        // If load balancer routes this request to Instance B → user not found
    }
}

// STATELESS (GOOD — any instance can handle any request):
@RestController
class OrderController {
    private final RedisTemplate<String, User> redis;  // Shared Redis

    @PostMapping("/login")
    public String login(@RequestBody LoginRequest req) {
        User user = authenticate(req);
        String sessionId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        redis.opsForValue().set("session:" + sessionId, user, Duration.ofHours(8));
        return sessionId;
        // Session stored in Redis — any instance can find it
    }

    @GetMapping("/orders")
    public List<Order> getOrders(@RequestHeader("Session-Id") String sessionId) {
        User user = redis.opsForValue().get("session:" + sessionId);
        // Works on any instance — all read from same Redis
    }
}
```

## Load Balancing Algorithms

```
ROUND ROBIN (default):
  Request 1 → Instance A
  Request 2 → Instance B
  Request 3 → Instance C
  Request 4 → Instance A (repeat)

  Good for: homogeneous requests with similar processing time
  Bad for: mixed load (some requests take 1ms, others take 5s)

LEAST CONNECTIONS:
  Route to instance with fewest active connections
  Good for: varying request duration
  Nginx: upstream { least_conn; server a; server b; server c; }

IP HASH (sticky sessions):
  hash(client_ip) % num_instances → always routes same IP to same server
  Good for: stateful services (shouldn't exist!), WebSocket connections
  Bad for: uneven distribution if many clients share an IP (NAT)

WEIGHTED:
  Instance A: weight=3 (newer, more powerful)
  Instance B: weight=1 (older)
  → 3:1 traffic ratio
  Good for: rolling upgrades, mixed server capacities
```

## Database Read Scaling with Replicas

```
ARCHITECTURE:
  All WRITES → Primary PostgreSQL
       ↓ (replication: sync or async)
  READS → Replica 1
          Replica 2
          Replica 3

JAVA ROUTING (AbstractRoutingDataSource):
```

```java
@Configuration
public class DataSourceConfig {

    @Bean
    public DataSource routingDataSource(
            @Qualifier("primaryDataSource") DataSource primary,
            @Qualifier("replicaDataSource") DataSource replica) {

        AbstractRoutingDataSource routing = new AbstractRoutingDataSource() {
            @Override
            protected Object determineCurrentLookupKey() {
                return TransactionSynchronizationManager.isCurrentTransactionReadOnly()
                    ? "replica" : "primary";
            }
        };
        routing.setTargetDataSources(Map.of("primary", primary, "replica", replica));
        routing.setDefaultTargetDataSource(primary);
        return routing;
    }
}

// Service usage:
@Transactional(readOnly = true)   // Routes to replica
public List<Order> getOrderHistory(Long userId) { ... }

@Transactional                     // Routes to primary (readOnly=false default)
public Order createOrder(CreateOrderRequest req) { ... }
```

## Auto-Scaling with Kubernetes HPA

```yaml
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: order-service-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: order-service
  minReplicas: 3        # Always run at least 3 (for HA)
  maxReplicas: 20       # Never exceed 20 (cost control)
  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 70  # Scale when avg CPU > 70%
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: memory
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 80
    - type: Pods
      pods:
        metric:
          name: http_requests_per_second  # Custom metric via Prometheus adapter
        target:
          type: AverageValue
          averageValue: "1000"  # Scale when RPS/pod > 1000
  behavior:
    scaleUp:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 60   # Don't scale up faster than every 60s
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300  # Don't scale down faster than every 5min
                                       # Prevents thrashing during traffic spikes
```

## Scaling Bottleneck Checklist

```
BOTTLENECK PROGRESSION (common order they hit):

1. Application tier (CPU bound) → Add more instances (HPA)
2. Application tier (I/O bound, waiting on DB) → Increase DB connection pool,
   add caching, optimize queries
3. Database reads → Add read replicas, add Redis cache
4. Database writes → Optimize write queries, batch inserts, event sourcing
5. Network bandwidth → CDN for large payloads, compression
6. Database writes (still) → Partitioning by time/range
7. Everything → Multi-region deployment
8. Database writes (severe) → Sharding (last resort)

MONITOR TO FIND YOUR BOTTLENECK:
  - CPU: high → scale or optimize algorithms
  - Memory: high → check for leaks, increase heap
  - DB connections: at max pool → slow queries or undersized pool
  - Cache miss rate: high → wrong TTL, missing indexes, wrong data cached
  - Error rate: rising → downstream dependency failing (add circuit breaker)
```

## Production Reality

For most Java backend services at intermediate scale (1M-10M users), the bottleneck is almost never the application tier — stateless Spring Boot services scale horizontally very easily. The bottleneck is almost always the database. Focus your scaling strategy there: add read replicas for read-heavy workloads, Redis caching for repeated queries, and index tuning for slow query paths. Auto-scaling handles traffic spikes for the app tier automatically with Kubernetes HPA — it's mostly configuration, not code.
